From f1bca9256d4730eff5092eaafedf8b248a4cd050 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tim Michals <tcmichals@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 19:46:20 -0500
Subject: [PATCH v3 4/7] remoteproc: sunxi: Add Allwinner A523 XuanTie E906
 driver

Add a remoteproc driver for the XuanTie E906 RISC-V core in the
Allwinner A523, A527 and T527 (sun55i).

.prepare() powers the RISC-V and SRAM power domains, releases the
configuration, SRAM and debug resets, enables the clocks, hands SRAM
space 1 to the core through bit 1 of the remap register and registers
the memory-region entries as carveouts. .start() writes the ELF entry
point to the boot address register and releases the core reset.

The driver translates the two SRAM windows, by core and by host
address; DRAM firmware, the trace buffer and the vrings are resolved by
the core from the carveouts. Vring kicks and the core's notifications
use one message box channel. The mailbox receive callback runs in
atomic context, so it queues work on an ordered workqueue, which calls
rproc_vq_interrupt(). An optional crash interrupt is reported with
rproc_report_crash().

Based on the vendor sunxi-rproc driver by Allwinner Technology Co.,
Ltd.

---
Changes in v3:
- The core is an E906 (RV32IMAFCX), not an E907
- The mailbox receive callback runs in atomic context; queue work on an
  ordered workqueue instead of calling rproc_vq_interrupt() from it
- da_to_va() translates only the two SRAM windows and returns NULL for
  anything else; DRAM, the trace buffer and the vrings come from the
  core's carveout lookup. Unmatched addresses are no longer used as
  host addresses (Sashiko)
- All register regions, clocks and resets are required; no fallback to
  the configuration reset for the core reset (Sashiko)
- Handle the debug reset and the RISC-V and SRAM power domains
- One mailbox channel for kicks and notifications
- Kicks send static const per-vring payloads with knows_txdone = false
  instead of a shared kick_msg (Sashiko)
- Teardown: rproc_del(), then mbox_free_channel(), then
  cancel_work_sync() (Sashiko)
- The crash interrupt is armed once the core reset is released, with an
  atomic flag (Sashiko)
- Read back the boot address before releasing the core reset (Sashiko)
- Carveouts mapped MEMREMAP_WC; SRAM regions reuse the SRAM mapping
  (Sashiko)
